7592186045417] [17592186184361 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184361 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184361 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184361 #edb/ref 1045 "Multiplying Mixed Numbers: Word Problems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184361 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186184895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184361 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184361 #edb/ref 1050 540.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184361 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "6d978d43-79f4-5343-ae3a-19ac497b011d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184545 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186181446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184545 #edb/ref 1069 "q-193605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184545 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184545 #edb/ref 1071 "Nancy uses $2\\frac{1}{4}$ cups of flour to make a dozen cupcakes. How many cups of flour does she need to prepare an order for $6$ dozen cupcakes?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184545 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e556544-bb4a-576d-8275-871131562662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184545 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the amount of flour that Nancy needs, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 6.$\n\nFirst, we write  $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($6$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1} & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{2}=13\\,\\text{R}\\,1=13\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, Nancy needs $13\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups of flour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184809 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184809 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60119" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184809 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184809 #edb/ref 1071 "Fred is $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{ft}$ tall. Julie is $\\frac{4}{5}$ of Fred's height. How tall is Julie?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184809 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "99bffa2e-8eed-5bab-aa2e-ce6936e0836c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184809 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out Julie's height, we need to calculate $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFirst, we write $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(4\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($2$) and the second fraction's numerator ($4$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{9}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}\\times \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 2}{5\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting this to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n18÷5=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, Julie is $3\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{ft}$ tall." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184818 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184818 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184818 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184818 #edb/ref 1071 "A box of cornflakes cereal weighs $1\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{oz}.$ Last week, Debby ate $2$ full boxes and another box which was only $\\frac{1}{4}$ full. How many ounces of this cereal did Debby eat in total?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1bd87de3-6afe-54f3-8697-30d835d51e37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184818 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that Debby had $2+\\frac{1}{4}=2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ boxes of cornflakes. So, to find out the total weight, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 2\\,\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(1\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 9}{2\\times 8}=\\frac{27}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{8}=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nSo, Debby ate a total of $3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{oz}$ of cornflakes cereal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184895 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184895 #edb/ref 1061 119844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184895 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10376ca0-4f49-5079-9693-b993a2a71af9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184895 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184896 #edb/ref 1061 119845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a65cb19-9690-5689-822f-24d2022ce76f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184896 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184897 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184897 #edb/ref 1061 119848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184897 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fcd48847-b6bc-5a18-9b7c-171dc644d3d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184897 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184898 #edb/ref 1061 119851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4252596e-a254-52eb-b4b5-0f01e9f6dffc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185379 #edb/ref 1064 "Multiplication of mixed fractions occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a country road is $5 \\, \\dfrac 12\\,\\textrm{km}$ long. If $\\dfrac 35$ of it is paved with asphalt, how long is the paved part?  \n\nTo find out the length of the paved part of the road, we need to calculate $5 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{3}{5}.$ \n\nFirst, we write $5 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n5 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{(5 \\times 2) + 1}{2} = \\dfrac{11}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{2} \\times \\dfrac{3}{5} =  \\dfrac{11 \\times 3}{2 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{33}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n33 \\div 10 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, the paved part of the road is $3\\,\\dfrac{3}{10}\\,\\textrm{km}$ long." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185379 #edb/ref 1065 9004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185379 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d51b839a-689a-5352-9c20-ec86045b9865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185379 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501488 #edb/ref 1080 "$6$ liters" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501488 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501488 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bce7c2c-3cb9-58c1-8a84-8000fa8f71af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186501505 #edb/ref 1080 "1\