rs?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "17d541e7-21d6-5b63-bfea-03f360c417cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502077 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out the number of chapters Daniel reads, we need to multiply $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ by $3.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(2\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($3$) and the second fraction's numerator ($3$) have a common factor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1} & =\\frac{7}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{1}\\times 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{1} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Daniel reads $7$ chapters in three hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502080 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502080 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502080 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502080 #edb/ref 1071 "A can of bitter lemon drink contains $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. If Phil drank $1$ whole can and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of a second can, how many grams of sugar did he drink?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502080 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "222813e8-d21a-59ba-b697-7bcb16bc7844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502080 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that Phil drank a total of $1+\\frac{3}{5}=1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ cans of the beverage. So, to find out how many grams of sugar he drank, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\times 1\\,\\frac{3}{5}.$    \n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+3}{5}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($5$) and the second fraction's denominator ($5$) have a common factor of $5.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{5} & =\\frac{5}{5}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,2=2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, Phil drank $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502091 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501999 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502091 #edb/ref 1069 "q-212001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502091 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502091 #edb/ref 1071 "A farmer has five $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$-acre fields. He planted wheat in $3$ fields and in $\\frac{1}{2}$ of another field. How many acres of wheat did the farmer plant?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502091 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35b950d1-e08b-5f6b-af7f-1977577a5d81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502091 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the farmer planted wheat in $3+\\frac{1}{2}=3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ fields. So, to find out the total acres, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 3\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{9\\times 7}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{63}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{63}{8}=7\\,\\text{R}\\,7=7\\,\\frac{7}{8}\n$$\n\nSo, the farmer planted $7\\,\\frac{7}{8}$ acres of wheat." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502099 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502099 #edb/ref 1069 "q-193555" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502099 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502099 #edb/ref 1071 "Mario fills three bottles with water. If the capacity of every bottle is $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ liters, how much water did he use to fill the bottles?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502099 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "42bf2d9b-a245-5919-95ec-686175cfea53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502099 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the total volume of water used to fill the bottles, we need to multiply $3$ by $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($3$) have a common factor of $3.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mario used $4$ liters of water to fill the bottles." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502121 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502121 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502121 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502121 #edb/ref 1071 "A country road is $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{mi}$ long. If $\\frac{3}{4}$ of it is paved with asphalt, how long is the paved part?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502121 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c7467de-330b-5939-83a9-4571e046a2da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502121 #edb/ref 1074 "To find out the length of the paved part of the road, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{4}.$ \n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(2\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5\\times 3}{2\\times 4}=\\frac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n15÷8=1\\,\\text{R}\\,7=1\\,\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the paved part of the road is $1\\,\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{mi}$ long." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
